00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:29,000
That sounds like a pretty full podcast. so let's get started, shall we?

52
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:34,000
Scout Master's ship in seven minutes or less.

53
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:50,000
The new guide to advancement that has just been published walks us through some of the thorny issues of evaluating active participation.

54
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:56,000
I've struggled at times, as many of us, with judging how to interpret the requirement that a scout be active,

55
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:02,000
And the team that assembled the new guide has made this process very simple and very clear,

56
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:09,000
And I'm not going to spend a tremendous amount of time talking about it, because evaluating active is a three step process now,

57
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:17,000
And if you go to the guide, there'll be a link to a PDF version on the post that contains this podcast.

58
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:24,000
You go to the guide, read the section and you'll see that there is a three step process

59
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:26,000
And this is the way it works.

60
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:29,000
Step number one: is the scout registered?

61
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:31,000
Easy enough, right?

62
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:38,000
I mean, that is going to be a 99.99% yes, right.

63
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:42,000
And then step number two: is the scout in good standing,

64
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:48,000
And that is also going to be a 99.99% yes.

65
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:52,000
And step number three: has he met the expectations of his unit?

66
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:56,000
That's a little more complex, but we're going to explain it very quickly.

67
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:59,000
The first two steps are cut and dry:

68
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:01,000
Good standing.

69
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:12,000
The only thing that good standing means- and this is it- is that a scout has not been excluded from membership by the unit, the council or the National Office of the BSA.

70
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:16,000
These are extraordinarily rare circumstances.

71
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:22,000
If a scout's been excluded from membership by anybody above the unit level, you will know that.

72
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:26,000
You will absolutely know that. it will be communicated to you.

73
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:28,000
Step three is also pretty simple.

74
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:32,000
Now. some scout units apply a metric to active

75
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:34,000
And I don't suggest that.

76
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:38,000
I think it's a very bad idea as a matter of fact, but it is a fact of life.

77
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,000
That's the way a lot of people do it

78
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:44,000
And that means a certain percentage of meetings and outings and things like that.

79
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:49,000
I've got lots of reasons why I don't think it's a particularly good idea.

80
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:51,000
A lot of people think that it works

81
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:58,000
To pass step three. if the scout meets that metric expectation, you're done.

82
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:00,000
He's been active.

83
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:10,000
In the vast majority of circumstances where you're going to be asked to evaluate the activity of a scout, those three tests are going to happen and it's not going to be a problem.

84
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:20,000
As I said, 99.9% of scouts are going to pass the first two and then 90% of them are going to pass number three and it's not a big deal.

85
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:25,000
So what if a boy hasn't met the stated expectations of his unit?

86
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:30,000
Well, if you read the guide to advancement you must have

87
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:33,000
Now. must is a very important word here.

88
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:36,000
Must It's not, may, should It's not suggested.

89
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:38,000
This is a policy statement.

90
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:45,000
It says you must have an alternative path to fulfilling the active requirement that recognizes influences outside of scouting.

91
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:53,000
These are very carefully described in the guide. to recognize that scouts are not able to control some circumstances in their life,

92
00:07:53,000 --> 00:08:01,000
We are also required to recognize activities outside of scouting that are cooperate with our aims and the values of scouting.

93
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:07,000
The end of the matter is that metrics are there in a lot of units.

94
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:15,000
Again, I'm going to emphasize that I don't think that's a great idea, but they alone cannot evaluate active.

95
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:21,000
We're not in competition with school and sports and church and worthwhile influences in a scout's life.

96
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:23,000
We're in cooperation with those things.

97
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:30,000
So if a boy doesn't pass the metric standard of active, then we have to sit and have a discussion,

98
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:35,000
We talk to him and we say: so, you didn't make the percentage of meetings and things like that.

99
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:36,000
Why is that?

100
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:40,000
I'm the president of the student body at school and I had some meetings I had to go to.

101
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:46,000
I'm involved in the FBLA, I'm involved in the YMCA, I'm involved in any number of things.

102
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:55,000
Now we're probably the only organization on the face of the earth that is going to allow our members to go out and participate in the community

103
00:08:55,000 --> 00:09:01,000
and recognize that as being active in our own organization, as that having impact on a scout's life,

104
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,000
And I think that's great.

105
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:12,000
The national advancement team has gone to great lengths to set out a concise, definitive set of standards that will put to rest

106
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:20,000
many of the things that we argue about and many of the things that cause scout masters and scouts a lot of ang.

107
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:26,000
And, as always, I want you to remember that the scout is usually his own best standard of if he's been active or not.

108
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:31,000
The way that I evaluate the active requirement, as I sit down with the scout and I say so,

109
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:36,000
says here: you have to be active in your troop and patrol for four months or whatever.

110
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:39,000
Do you think you've met that requirement?

111
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:42,000
And if you do think so, then tell me why.

112
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:44,000
If you don't think so, then tell me why

113
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:46,000
We're going to enter into discussion about it.

114
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:48,000
The scout does not have to impress me.

115
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:51,000
The scout does not have to prove something to me.

116
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:59,000
What we have to do is we have to find out- him and I on the same side of the question, if he's fulfilled the requirement.

117
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:01,000
There you go,

118
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:03,000
Three tests for active.

119
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:04,000
Number one: be registered.

120
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:11,000
Number two: don't be excluded by either the unit or the council or the national office.

121
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:14,000
Number three: meet the expectations of your troop.

122
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:17,000
There's been a reasonable amount of back and forth about this on the blog.

123
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:20,000
Some people like the idea of the standards written in the guide.

124
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:22,000
Some people aren't too keen about them.

125
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:24,000
Some people think that they're very bad indeed.

126
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:27,000
This doesn't really change anything that I'm doing.

127
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:34,000
I think that we had it right to begin with, and I'm not saying that to pretend that I'm the smartest man in the world.

128
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:39,000
You may need to adjust the way that you're handling this,

129
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:46,000
But what I really appreciate about this is the inclusion of other community activities and the consideration of active.

130
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:49,000
I think it's a brilliant idea and I would love to hear your ideas about it.

131
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:53,000
So do get in touch and you'll find out how to do that towards the end of the podcast.

