00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:37,000
or help prepare a meal for a minimum of an hour.

227
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:40,000
There's no test for anything but participation there.

228
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:42,000
He does it once and he's completed the requirement,

229
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
Easy, right.

230
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:47,000
The second requirement reads a little differently.

231
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:50,000
It says: demonstrate that you know how.

232
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:53,000
Demonstrate that you know how

233
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:57,000
Well. that means skill or knowledge that a Scout's proficient in,

234
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:59,000
Not something that he did just once.

235
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:03,000
Once Scout comes to you, he wants you to check off the requirement for two half-hs.

236
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,000
He ties the knot in two seconds.

237
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:10,000
Once Scout comes to you, it takes him 60 seconds and he biffs it up a couple of times,

238
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:13,000
But they both satisfy the requirement right.

239
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:16,000
If they know how to tie the knot, you don't have to coach them through it.

240
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:19,000
They know how to tie the knot.

241
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:23,000
Now, if the boy comes to you and spends five minutes trying to figure out how to tie the knot-

242
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:26,000
well, does he know how to do it or not?

243
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:28,000
In my book he doesn't know how to do it,

244
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:33,000
So I don't call him a failure and send him off into the outer darkness.

245
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:38,000
It's like: okay, so you need to practice on that a little bit more.

246
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:41,000
If there's somebody that you can practice with- hey, one of the guys here-

247
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:44,000
they are pretty good at tying knots- just practice it a few times,

248
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:46,000
There's no big deal.

249
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:50,000
And then, when you know exactly how to tie that knot, come back to me and show me

250
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:53,000
and we'll sign the requirement off.

251
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:58,000
We need to look at signing requirements and boards of review

252
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:01,000
and Scout Master Conferences and things like that as a dialogue.

253
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:06,000
We have a dialogue with Scouts and we both decide if the Scout's ready.

254
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:08,000
Is he ready to have a requirement signed off?

255
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:11,000
Is he ready to move on to the next rank?

256
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:17,000
But reading the requirement and making sure we both understand what's supposed to happen

257
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:21,000
is going to take us forward in a very positive manner.

258
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:24,000
Now, like most adults who get involved in scouting

259
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:29,000
somewhere along the way, I got upset that my Scouts didn't seem to know their skills.

260
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:32,000
Everybody encounters this at one point.

261
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
Now that leads us to react in several different ways.

262
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:39,000
Me, I kind of started to crack down on things and I started requiring all kinds of things

263
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:44,000
that the actual written requirements of the Scout handbook don't.

264
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:46,000
Then we start thinking about fairness.

265
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:49,000
Well, how can I require one Scout to tie a knot in 10 seconds

266
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:52,000
and let another one go if he takes 30 seconds?

267
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:56,000
And then we sit down and we have an extra cup of coffee and we start writing

268
00:19:56,000 --> 00:20:01,000
And I have seen pages and pages of different requirement standards that people have written in-

269
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:04,000
And you know it's all reasonably good-hearted-

270
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:07,000
and that they're trying to standardize things and they're trying to make it work,

271
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:11,000
But I got to tell you that's not scouting.

272
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:13,000
That's really not scouting.

273
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:17,000
What most of us end up discovering in the end is that advancement is only an indication

274
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:20,000
of how well the program is impacting the Scout.

275
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:26,000
It's only part of the picture and it's based on the effort of individual Scouts.

276
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:29,000
When youth leaders- or adults for that matter- sign off requirements,

277
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:31,000
there's learning on both sides of the equation.

278
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:33,000
It's just not proficiency we're looking for.

279
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:35,000
It's overall growth and participation.

280
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:39,000
It's successively more challenging activities and skills.

281
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:42,000
Scouts advance when they do the things that Scouts do

282
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:45,000
not, because they satisfy requirements.

283
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:48,000
So I would encourage you to stop thinking about requirements

284
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:51,000
and start thinking about how the program reaches an individual Scout.

285
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:54,000
This can be a little difficult because we usually get off on the wrong foot

286
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:59,000
in Cubs, where everything seems to be about earning a patch or a belt loop.

287
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:02,000
Boys pick up on that quickly and they start looking at everything they do

288
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:05,000
as a way to get the next shiny object

289
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:08,000
And, being boys, they start to find the quickest route to being able to say

290
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:09,000
I'm done.

291
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:12,000
A lot of times we get so focused on checking off lists

292
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:15,000
that we're in danger of missing the bigger picture.

293
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:18,000
As for uniform standards, in a troop

294
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:21,000
about one person should be signing off the requirement

295
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:23,000
with exactly the same standard as the other person.

296
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:26,000
I don't think that that's very important. to tell you the truth,

297
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:29,000
Did the Scout satisfy someone empowered to sign the requirement?

298
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:31,000
That's all that's needed.

299
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:34,000
When you go to Scout Camp, you're going to check in at the pool

300
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:36,000
and you're going to do a swim test.

301
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:38,000
So watch the swim test with this in mind sometime.

302
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:41,000
The counselors: what do they want out of the swim test?

303
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:44,000
They want to be sure that a Scout can swim a given distance,

304
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:47,000
that he knows a resting stroke, for instance, that he can tread water.

305
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:50,000
Beyond that, it's not about uniformity at all.

306
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:53,000
A Scout on the swim team at the local pool will jump in

307
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:55,000
and cuts through the water like a pro.

308
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:58,000
A Scout who'd rather be doing anything else than swimming

309
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:02,000
is going to jump in and splash and sputter his way through the test.

310
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,000
Maybe he'll finish it, maybe he won't,

311
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:07,000
But if he finishes it he completes it.

312
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:09,000
So does the guy on the swim team.

313
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:11,000
Did the swim team guy do a better job?

314
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:14,000
Yeah, probably if you were judging it that way.

315
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:18,000
But the idea is the impact on the Scout right.

316
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:21,000
The idea is the impact on the Scout

317
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:23,000
Did our guy who jumped in and sputtered his way through

318
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:26,000
and kind of splashed a lot, he finished- it

319
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:31,000
Was his effort, any less than the guy on the swim team.

320
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:32,000
Maybe his effort was more,

321
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:34,000
Maybe he had more to overcome there.

322
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:37,000
So advancement very highly individualized.

323
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:39,000
And if that doesn't convince you,

324
00:22:39,000 --> 00:22:42,000
think about that test at 100 different camps.

325
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:44,000
They're all doing it exactly the same right.

326
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:45,000
No way.

327
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:47,000
I mean they're using the same standard,

328
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:50,000
But the counselors at 100 different camps

329
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:53,000
are not going to administer the test exactly the same way

330
00:22:53,000 --> 00:22:55,000
and off of the same script.

331
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:56,000
No,

332
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:58,000
The end result is Scouts have demonstrated

333
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:00,000
they can safely use the pool.

334
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:02,000
That's all that really matters in the end.

335
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:05,000
If we observed and evaluated the same test in 100 camps,

336
00:23:05,000 --> 00:23:07,000
we'd probably determine that in some camps

337
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:10,000
it was a little harder to pass the test than it is in others,

338
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:12,000
But that there were common elements in the way

339
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:14,000
each test was administered.

340
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:15,000
Now, if that doesn't come in,

341
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:17,000
you should start thinking about every Scout

342
00:23:17,000 --> 00:23:19,000
taking any merit badge in a given year.

343
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:22,000
Now how many differences in instruction and evaluation

344
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:25,000
are there going to be? for you know,

345
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:29,000
40,000 Scouts working with 40,000 different counselors

346
00:23:29,000 --> 00:23:31,000
earning the same merit badge,

347
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:34,000
Should we attempt to judge which Scout

348
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:36,000
did a better job of the merit badge

349
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:39,000
or which counselor did a better job of instruction?

350
00:23:39,000 --> 00:23:41,000
I really don't think so.

351
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:44,000
As long as a Scout satisfies somebody

352
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:48,000
empowered to sign the requirement,

353
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:50,000
that's enough.

354
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:52,000
Uniformity to me not important.

355
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:54,000
What's important is how the program

356
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:56,000
reaches the individual Scout.

357
